    "file": "php-src/Zend/zend_vm_execute.h",
    "description": "In the array append path of ZEND_ASSIGN_DIM_SPEC_VAR_UNUSED_OP_DATA_VAR_HANDLER, the handler fetches `value` from OP_DATA as IS_VAR, dereferences it (`ZVAL_DEREF(value)`), and may then destroy the original temporary (`zval_ptr_dtor_nogc(free_op_data)`). If `value` points into the same ref container as `free_op_data` (e.g., through a reference produced earlier by ZEND_MAKE_REF), this dtor can free the referenced zend_reference. The code then still uses `value` for `ZVAL_COPY(EX_VAR(opline->result.var), value)` when the assignment result is used, causing a heap-use-after-free read. So the root bug is lifetime mismanagement in this handler: using `value` after potentially freeing its backing ref via `free_op_data` destruction.",
    "conditions": "Triggered by ASSIGN_DIM with OP_DATA of type VAR in append mode (`[]=`) where RETURN_VALUE_USED(opline) is true, and where OP_DATA VAR is a reference/alias setup such that `ZVAL_DEREF(value)` yields a pointer into the same zend_reference that `free_op_data` owns. Data flow in this PoV: fuzzed script executes bytecode that first creates a reference (seen via ZEND_MAKE_REF), then performs ASSIGN_DIM ... OP_DATA_VAR; handler inserts value, destroys free_op_data, and later copies `value` to result, reading freed memory."
